lesson chemistry-year-review-5 v=1 format=review grade=5 unit=9 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Chemistry review: the fifth year"
  goal "Look back over the year: dissolving and distilling, rust, elements and air, heating curves, metals, mortar and the signs of a new substance."

  warm-up {
    practice chem.which-saturated count=1 seed=2
    practice chem.ringed-number count=1 seed=1
    practice chem.rock-for-lime count=1 seed=2
  }
  exercises {
    practice chem.solubility-table count=1 seed=1 hard-seed=2
    practice chem.drip-rate count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=4 hard-seed=10
    practice chem.paper-keeps count=1 seed=1 hard-seed=2
    practice chem.rust-pair count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=2
    practice chem.how-many-elements count=1 seed=3 easy-seed=7 hard-seed=7
    practice chem.breath-table count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=7 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.curve-flat-temp count=1 seed=2
    practice chem.magnet-not-test count=1 seed=7
    practice chem.slaked-lime count=1 seed=1 hard-seed=2
    practice chem.fizz-cooler count=1 seed=1 hard-seed=2
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    practice chem.scratched-coat count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=2
  }
  puzzle stars=3 {
    practice chem.pour-together count=1 seed=1
  }

  grown-ups """
    This is a check, not a test, to do at the end of the fifth year. The warm-up is the first
    question of three lessons: the saturated beaker, the ringed cell of the table of elements, and
    the rock that makes lime. The exercises take one question each from the year: the table of
    grams that dissolve, the drips collected from the still, what filter paper keeps back, the
    fair pair of rust jars, how many elements a box of molecules holds, the air breathed in and
    out, where a heating curve goes flat, why a magnet is not a test for a metal, the lime and water
    that get hot, and how much colder the fizzing vinegar gets. Anything that needs the way in again
    is worth another week with that lesson, and everything else can move on. The two puzzles are
    there to be enjoyed: why a scratched zinc coat still protects the iron, and how many grams stay
    as grains when two beakers of salty water are poured together. Ask for the reason as well as the
    answer.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level each exercise is drawn from the plainer versions its lesson asks at its own easier level, and the two puzzles are the plainer ones: the coat that keeps water and air off, and two beakers that end with no grains."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level each exercise is drawn from the versions its lesson asks at its harder level, with amounts worked backwards and larger numbers, and the puzzles are a scratch through the zinc and beakers of 100 ml each."
  }
}
